      "## Question and scope\n\nThe study measures the record and the handoff, not the professional judgment of QA lead or the legal outcome of the work. State how duplicate requests are identified and whether they are merged, linked, or counted separately. Distinguish an administrative pause from a failure: a pause waits for a named decision, while a failure leaves the next action unowned. Keep the protocol with the deployment window batch so the next reviewer can see what was measured. Use the same note in the case file and the study extract; do not create a second version in a personal document. The deployment window review at Outsourced Programmers should state whether the rollback plan was current on October 28.\n\nThe protocol does not estimate an industry benchmark; it produces a local description that engineering managers can review. The unit definition should be stable enough that two reviewers can classify the same case the same way. Distinguish an administrative pause from a failure: a pause waits for a named decision, while a failure leaves the next action unowned. Record the exclusions in advance so a difficult case cannot be removed after the result is known. Keep the measurement note short enough to read with the case and specific enough to support the reported dependency age. The October 28 deployment window note should distinguish a missing QA evidence from a confirmed negative.",
      "## Population and unit\n\nIf a reopened case creates a new requirement, count it as a new case and retain a link to the earlier record. The population should include unresolved cases, not only closures, or the study will overstate completion. Define how a case that waits for engineering manager is counted: paused, open, or excluded, but not silently dropped. Separate cases opened before the window from cases opened and closed inside it, and report both counts. Link the observation to the exact field in the deployment console and to the QA evidence that supports it. The engineering manager decision on deployment window should be dated October 28 and linked to the QA evidence.\n\nWhen several ticket updates belong to one request, state whether the request or each update is the unit and apply that rule consistently. Record the queue at the start and end of each observation day so additions and withdrawals are visible. Define how a case that waits for developer is counted: paused, open, or excluded, but not silently dropped. If a request was reopened after the window, keep it in the original population and report the later event separately. If the case moved between owners, include both owner periods in the review note and name the handoff time. The October 28 deployment window close should name the destination field, the approver, and the escaped defect rate check.",
      "## Definitions and measures\n\nA pause should have a reason, an owner, and a review date; otherwise it is an unowned case with a different label. Use a stable case key and a separate identity crosswalk stored in an approved system. Define whether a case that waits for a third party is open, paused, or excluded, and apply that rule to every owner. Keep a change log for every definition adjustment and the date it took effect. When a value is missing, record the missing state and the reason instead of leaving an empty field in the extract. The October 28 deployment window exception log should show the reason code, owner, and release decision.\n\nMeasure elapsed time from the first recorded request to the verified destination update, and report waiting intervals separately. Keep the original text and the coded value together so a reviewer can check the classification. Record the person who approved the definition and the date it became effective. Give one concrete example and one boundary case to each reviewer before collection begins. If the evidence is sensitive, record the approved reference and access path rather than copying the content. The October 28 deployment window recovery test should record the restore point and the deployment failure rate result.",
      "## Data dictionary and evidence\n\nEach case needs the source, the observation time, the field supported, the owner, and the reviewer. Keep the identity crosswalk in the approved system and restrict it to the review owner. Keep the source reference and the coded value in the same row, and do not overwrite the original with a later correction. Archive the dictionary with the study so a later reviewer can reproduce the categories. Record this decision in the study log with the case key, the review date, and the person who approved the classification. The October 28 control note for Outsourced Programmers should name the deployment window source, the owner, and the next review time.\n\nThe data dictionary should name the allowed values for status, exception reason, owner, and close evidence. Name the required fields for each article type and mark conditional fields in the dictionary. Run a five-case pilot before the full sample and revise any field that two reviewers interpret differently. Keep the original request, the approval, and the destination value linked to the same key. If the source changes after the observation, preserve the earlier value and add a dated note instead of replacing it. The October 28 evidence trail for deployment window should show the source version and the developer decision.\n\nKeep free-text notes separate from coded fields so the analysis does not depend on inconsistent wording. Version the dictionary and report which version was used for each observation period. Keep the original evidence reference next to the coded value so the reviewer can verify the classification. Keep the original request, the approval, and the destination value linked to the same key. Use the same note in the case file and the study extract; do not create a second version in a personal document. The deployment window review at Outsourced Programmers should state whether the deployment window was current on October 28.",
      "## Sampling and collection\n\nInclude adverse cases: a missing code review, a conflicting source, an unavailable QA lead, and a tight deadline. Collect the exception reason and the action taken before the close so the analysis can separate detection from resolution. Use a random or systematic start rule and then take consecutive cases within each stratum. Record the sampling frame as an export with a date, filter, and row count. Keep the measurement note short enough to read with the case and specific enough to support the reported access exceptions. The October 28 deployment window note should distinguish a missing code review from a confirmed negative.\n\nUse a random or systematic start rule for the first case, then take consecutive cases within each stratum. Keep the collection window and the number of collectors in the report; both affect how the result should be interpreted. Keep the sample selection reproducible by recording the export date, filter, and random seed or start rule. Use a random start and a fixed interval when the frame is ordered, and record both. Link the observation to the exact field in the code repository and to the deployment window that supports it. The product owner decision on deployment window should be dated October 28 and linked to the deployment window.\n\nUse consecutive deployment window cases where possible, then stratify by owner, channel, and risk class. Collect the case from the CI pipeline, the rollback plan, and the approval record, and stop at the verified close. A sample of recent closures can miss the oldest unresolved work, which is often the most informative. Record the date of the sample and the version of the queue export. If the case moved between owners, include both owner periods in the review note and name the handoff time. The October 28 deployment window close should name the destination field, the approver, and the review turnaround check.",
      "## Analysis and reporting\n\nReport the eligible count, the sample, the missing-field rate, the exception rate, and the unresolved count at the close of the window. Report denominators with every rate and separate missing evidence from negative evidence. Record the sample date, the denominator, the numerator, and the exclusion count for every reported rate. Use a case-level table to explain an outlier before treating it as a process signal. When a value is missing, record the missing state and the reason instead of leaving an empty field in the extract. The October 28 deployment window exception log should show the reason code, owner, and release decision.\n\nSeparate observation from interpretation; a high completion rate on a small easy sample is not evidence of a strong control. The analysis should end with the decision the evidence can support and the decision it cannot support. Show the unresolved cases by owner, age, and dependency instead of reporting a single completion percentage. When a rate changes, check whether the case mix or the evidence standard changed at the same time. If the evidence is sensitive, record the approved reference and access path rather than copying the content. The October 28 deployment window recovery test should record the restore point and the deployment failure rate result.",
      "## Limitations\n\nThe study cannot determine whether the professional decision was correct, only whether the record and handoff are reconstructable. The record may be incomplete or delayed, so the method cannot infer work that never entered the queue. If the reviewer also performed the work, note the independence limitation and use a second reviewer for a subsample. Record the access problems that prevented a case from being reviewed. Record this decision in the study log with the case key, the review date, and the person who approved the classification. The October 28 control note for Outsourced Programmers should name the deployment window source, the owner, and the next review time.\n\nReport what remains unknown and what evidence would be needed to answer the next question. A clean summary can hide missing evidence, especially when the operator who did the work is also the reviewer. If the reviewer also performed the work, note the independence limitation and use a second reviewer for a subsample. A missing record is evidence about the record, not proof that the work was never done. If the source changes after the observation, preserve the earlier value and add a dated note instead of replacing it. The October 28 evidence trail for deployment window should show the source version and the product owner decision.",
      "## Review and next action\n\nChoose one next action: fix the source, simplify the QA evidence, change the queue, adjust access, or pause the work until the owner decides. If the action changes failed deployment, record the approval from engineering manager before implementation. Choose a measure that can move within one cycle and a second measure that checks for unintended effects. Link the action to the case evidence that prompted it, not only to the meeting notes. Use the same note in the case file and the study extract; do not create a second version in a personal document. The deployment window review at Outsourced Programmers should state whether the QA evidence was current on October 28.\n\nRepeat the protocol after the change with the same definitions, window, and exclusions. Keep the review note with the deployment window evidence so the next owner can see why the process changed. Close the review only when the owner has accepted the result and the next check is scheduled. Report a failed action with the same detail as a successful one so the team can learn from it. Keep the measurement note short enough to read with the case and specific enough to support the reported escaped defect rate. The October 28 deployment window note should distinguish a missing ticket from a confirmed negative."
