      "## Question and scope\n\nWrite the primary question and one supporting question, then stop; a long list of questions usually hides an undefined unit. A count without a denominator is not a measurement, especially when the queue changes size during the day. If a definition changes during the review, keep the earlier classification and apply the new rule only to later cases. Write the decision the study is meant to inform before choosing the measure. When a value is missing, record the missing state and the reason instead of leaving an empty field in the extract. The October 28 ticket intake exception log should show the reason code, owner, and release decision.\n\nThe study measures the record and the handoff, not the professional judgment of developer or the legal outcome of the work. Record the opening count, additions, withdrawals, resolutions, and unresolved items at the end of the window. The definitions should be short enough to use at the queue and precise enough to defend in a review with developer. The protocol should be short enough for an operator to follow and specific enough for a reviewer to repeat. If the evidence is sensitive, record the approved reference and access path rather than copying the content. The October 28 ticket intake recovery test should record the restore point and the deployment failure rate result.",
