      "## Question and scope\n\nThe protocol does not estimate an industry benchmark; it produces a local description that engineering managers can review. Record the opening count, additions, withdrawals, resolutions, and unresolved items at the end of the window. Define the start event, the stop event, the pause rule, and the treatment of a missing code review before collecting data. Write the decision the study is meant to inform before choosing the measure. When a value is missing, record the missing state and the reason instead of leaving an empty field in the extract. The October 28 dependency update exception log should show the reason code, owner, and release decision.\n\nA useful scope is narrow enough to reproduce and specific enough to show whether failed deployment is being detected. The unit definition should be stable enough that two reviewers can classify the same case the same way. Distinguish an administrative pause from a failure: a pause waits for a named decision, while a failure leaves the next action unowned. If the study is exploratory, label it exploratory and do not present the result as a stable baseline. If the evidence is sensitive, record the approved reference and access path rather than copying the content. The October 28 dependency update recovery test should record the restore point and the escaped defect rate result.",
      "## Population and unit\n\nRecord the opening count by owner and channel, because a single total can hide a queue concentrated in one person. Report the number of cases that could not be classified and the reason, rather than forcing them into a category. If the definition of review turnaround changes, keep the old and new values side by side for the transition period. A case that never received an owner still belongs in the population; do not drop it because it is difficult to classify. Record this decision in the study log with the case key, the review date, and the person who approved the classification. The October 28 control note for Outsourced Programmers should name the dependency update source, the owner, and the next review time.\n\nIf a reopened case creates a new requirement, count it as a new case and retain a link to the earlier record. If duplicate requests are merged, keep the duplicate count and the merge rule in the dataset. Write the rule for a missing rollback plan before the first case so the treatment is consistent. If a request was reopened after the window, keep it in the original population and report the later event separately. If the source changes after the observation, preserve the earlier value and add a dated note instead of replacing it. The October 28 evidence trail for dependency update should show the source version and the product owner decision.",
      "## Definitions and measures\n\nTest each definition on a routine case, a missing-evidence case, and a case that crossed owners. Define allowed values for status, reason, owner, and evidence state before collection. A duplicate request is a separate intake event even when it points to the same underlying need; record the link. If two reviewers disagree, preserve both classifications until the definition is clarified. Use the same note in the case file and the study extract; do not create a second version in a personal document. The dependency update review at Outsourced Programmers should state whether the rollback plan was current on October 28.\n\nDefine how a correction after close is linked to the original case and whether it creates a new case. A blank field, a not-applicable field, and a confirmed negative field should have different codes. A correction is a change to a closed record; a clarification that does not change the record is a note, not a correction. State how duplicate, cancelled, and reopened cases enter the numerator and denominator. Keep the measurement note short enough to read with the case and specific enough to support the reported dependency age. The October 28 dependency update note should distinguish a missing QA evidence from a confirmed negative.",
      "## Data dictionary and evidence\n\nTest the dictionary on five cases before the full sample; ambiguous fields should be fixed before collection, not after. Use a controlled list for exception reasons; do not let free text become the analysis category. Store sensitive evidence in the approved repository and keep only the reference in the analysis file. Keep the original request, the approval, and the destination value linked to the same key. Link the observation to the exact field in the code repository and to the QA evidence that supports it. The QA lead decision on dependency update should be dated October 28 and linked to the QA evidence.\n\nEach case needs the source, the observation time, the field supported, the owner, and the reviewer. Test the dictionary on a case with a missing ticket and a case with a corrected value. Store sensitive evidence in the approved repository and keep only the reference in the analysis file. Record the source location, not just the file name, so a reviewer can reopen the exact evidence. If the case moved between owners, include both owner periods in the review note and name the handoff time. The October 28 dependency update close should name the destination field, the approver, and the escaped defect rate check.\n\nIf the source is a document, record the page or field; if the source is a system, record the record identifier and extraction time. Test the dictionary on a case with a missing pull request and a case with a corrected value. Store sensitive evidence in the approved repository and keep only the reference in the analysis file. Do not use a zero or an empty string to represent missing evidence. When a value is missing, record the missing state and the reason instead of leaving an empty field in the extract. The October 28 dependency update exception log should show the reason code, owner, and release decision.",
      "## Sampling and collection\n\nUse consecutive dependency update cases where possible, then stratify by owner, channel, and risk class. Collect the case from the CI pipeline, the ticket, and the approval record, and stop at the verified close. Do not expand the sample only because the early result looks weak or strong. A case that is difficult to access is still part of the frame; report the access failure rather than replacing it silently. If the evidence is sensitive, record the approved reference and access path rather than copying the content. The October 28 dependency update recovery test should record the restore point and the review turnaround result.\n\nSample across the full observation window, including the days when the queue was busiest and the days when coverage changed. Record the time of each observation so a later reviewer can distinguish a late entry from a changed decision. Keep the sample selection reproducible by recording the export date, filter, and random seed or start rule. Record the sampling frame as an export with a date, filter, and row count. Record this decision in the study log with the case key, the review date, and the person who approved the classification. The October 28 control note for Outsourced Programmers should name the dependency update source, the owner, and the next review time.\n\nInclude adverse cases: a missing code review, a conflicting source, an unavailable QA lead, and a tight deadline. Collect the case from the dependency scanner, the code review, and the approval record, and stop at the verified close. A sample of recent closures can miss the oldest unresolved work, which is often the most informative. Record the date of the sample and the version of the queue export. If the source changes after the observation, preserve the earlier value and add a dated note instead of replacing it. The October 28 evidence trail for dependency update should show the source version and the QA lead decision.",
      "## Analysis and reporting\n\nCompare rollback readiness across owners only after checking the case mix, the channel, and the evidence standard. For dependency update, report the age distribution and the share of cases waiting on a named owner. Report the number of cases that changed status after the first extraction and how the change was handled. End with the decision the evidence supports and the decision it does not support. Use the same note in the case file and the study extract; do not create a second version in a personal document. The dependency update review at Outsourced Programmers should state whether the pull request was current on October 28.\n\nState which findings are descriptive and which require a follow-up test with a larger or different sample. The analysis should end with the decision the evidence can support and the decision it cannot support. Report the number of cases that changed status after the first extraction and how the change was handled. When a rate changes, check whether the case mix or the evidence standard changed at the same time. Keep the measurement note short enough to read with the case and specific enough to support the reported dependency age. The October 28 dependency update note should distinguish a missing code review from a confirmed negative.",
      "## Limitations\n\nSmall samples are sensitive to one case; report the instability instead of presenting a percentage as a stable rate. The cited sources describe recordkeeping, privacy, security, or control practice; they do not supply a local benchmark. Record the response or access problems that prevented a case from being reviewed. Record the access problems that prevented a case from being reviewed. Link the observation to the exact field in the dependency scanner and to the code review that supports it. The engineering manager decision on dependency update should be dated October 28 and linked to the code review.\n\nThe cited public sources frame recordkeeping, privacy, security, or control practice; they do not provide a local result. The cited sources describe recordkeeping, privacy, security, or control practice; they do not supply a local benchmark. Report the limits of the incident channel data, including fields that were unavailable or changed during the window. Record the access problems that prevented a case from being reviewed. If the case moved between owners, include both owner periods in the review note and name the handoff time. The October 28 dependency update close should name the destination field, the approver, and the escaped defect rate check.",
      "## Review and next action\n\nRecord the decision and the next check so the study does not end as an archive. Name the owner, the review date, and the decision the study is expected to inform. If the action requires a policy or access change, route it to the owner and record the decision. Close the review when the owner accepts the finding and the next test is on the calendar. When a value is missing, record the missing state and the reason instead of leaving an empty field in the extract. The October 28 dependency update exception log should show the reason code, owner, and release decision.\n\nIf the action affects stale dependency, route it to product owner and record the approval before implementation. Repeat the protocol with the same definitions and window, then compare the same measures. Report the action result even if the change did not work; a failed test is still evidence. Link the action to the case evidence that prompted it, not only to the meeting notes. If the evidence is sensitive, record the approved reference and access path rather than copying the content. The October 28 dependency update recovery test should record the restore point and the deployment failure rate result."
